 ## Overview
-Transformer-based architectures replace or augment the standard Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLPs) in radiance field representations with self-attention mechanisms to capture long-range dependencies and global context [S3, unofficial]. Unlike standard NeRF variants that rely on local spectral bias mitigation via positional encoding and per-scene optimization, these methods leverage attention to aggregate information across multiple views or spatial extents, enabling generalizable scene reconstruction and efficient super-resolution [S1, S3, unofficial].
+Transformer-based architectures replace the local, per-scene Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLPs) of standard NeRF with global attention mechanisms to aggregate information from multiple source views. This enables generalizable radiance field learning, allowing the model to infer geometry and appearance for unseen scenes without per-scene optimization [S1, unofficial][S2]. Unlike sibling methods that may use transformers for specific tasks (e.g., refinement or editing), this family fundamentally restructures the scene representation to be view-aggregating and dataset-trained [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family share the mechanism of mapping 6D input coordinates to 4D output values using a differentiable neural network, allowing the radiance field to be optimized end-to-end through the volumetric rendering integral.
