 ## Background
-The Transformer architecture, originally introduced for sequence modeling, computes pairwise attention over input tokens to capture long-range dependencies [S3, unofficial]. In the context of volume rendering, Vision Transformers (ViT) adapt this by treating image patches as tokens [S3, unofficial]. The application of transformers to NeRF began with efforts to improve generalization and computational efficiency. The "Transformer Proposer" was introduced to reduce the computational cost of attention mechanisms in sampling proposals by constraining the architecture to single transformer blocks with low-dimensional projections [S1, unofficial]. Subsequent work explored Masked Autoencoders (MAE) combined with 3D transformers to learn generalized representations from sparse inputs, enabling transfer learning to unseen scenes [S2]. More recently, transformers have been applied to volume super-resolution and denoising, leveraging their global receptive field to recover large-scale structures that local convolutions fail to capture [S3, unofficial].
+The application of Transformers to volume rendering emerged as a solution to the computational bottleneck of per-scene optimization in NeRF. Standard NeRF requires hours of optimization per scene, limiting its utility for large-scale or real-time applications [S1, unofficial]. By leveraging the self-attention mechanism, which captures long-range dependencies that convolutions miss, researchers adapted the Transformer architecture to aggregate features from multiple source views [S3, unofficial]. This shift enabled the development of generalizable radiance fields, where a single model trained on a dataset of scenes can infer new scenes without iterative optimization [S1, unofficial][S2]. The use of Masked Autoencoders (MAE) further advanced this by allowing self-supervised learning of 3D representations from sparse inputs [S2].
 
